# Ask Sunny — environment variables.
#
# PLACEHOLDERS ONLY. Never commit real values.
# Copy to .env.local (which is gitignored) and fill it in there.
#
# Variables prefixed NEXT_PUBLIC_ are inlined into the browser bundle and are
# therefore PUBLIC. Everything else is server-only and must never be given a
# NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Mode -----
# Read through isDemoMode() in src/lib/config/runtime.ts, which is the only
# place this variable is interpreted. Three states, and they are exhaustive:
#
#   "false"  live mode: Supabase + Anthropic are required, and a missing one
#            produces an error rather than a demo answer. The login screen
#            offers no demo entry, so nobody can sign in until a real identity
#            provider is connected.
#   "true"   demo mode: seeded content, mock provider, no external services.
#   unset    demo mode, identical to "true".
#
# The word is matched CASE-INSENSITIVELY and surrounding whitespace is ignored,
# so "false", "False", "FALSE" and " false " all select live mode. Anything that
# is not the word false — "0", "no", "off", a typo — is demo, so a genuinely
# misspelled variable still fails safe rather than pointing a prototype at live
# services.
#
# Salon Performance is the exception and does not consult this at all: it reads
# real reporting data from Supabase in either mode.
NEXT_PUBLIC_DEMO_MODE=true

# ON A REAL VERCEL PRODUCTION DEPLOYMENT THIS VARIABLE DECIDES NOTHING.
# Production is live, whatever it says. Preview, development and local are
# unaffected and read it exactly as described above.
#
# WHY. NEXT_PUBLIC_ variables are substituted into the bundle at BUILD time, so
# a deployment behaves by whatever value the dashboard held when that build ran
# — editing it afterwards changes nothing until a rebuild, and no code can read
# the runtime value back. Production served the seeded demo for exactly that
# reason while every credential was present and working, which is not a
# cosmetic failure: demo mode answers with the mock provider, which invents
# policy, and puts the demo role switcher on the login screen, which lets any
# visitor pick a manager role. Production is the one place neither is
# acceptable, so it no longer depends on a string that can go stale.
#
# The signal it uses instead is NEXT_PUBLIC_VERCEL_ENV, which Vercel sets
# itself per deployment. Nothing to configure — but it does require "Automatically
# expose System Environment Variables" to stay enabled in Project Settings,
# which is the Vercel default.

# OPTIONAL, and normally unset. Set to exactly "true" to run a PRODUCTION
# deployment in demo mode anyway — the deliberate way back, so the rule above
# is reversible without a code change. It must be set at BUILD time like every
# other NEXT_PUBLIC_ variable. Anything other than "true" leaves Production live.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_ALLOW_DEMO_IN_PRODUCTION=

# ----------------------------------------------- Business timezone ---------
# WHICH DAY THE BUSINESS IS IN. Read through src/lib/business-date.ts, which is
# the only place this variable is interpreted, and the only answer three
# separate features take for what "today" is: whether a coaching follow-up is
# overdue, what the Overview counts as due this week, and how far behind today
# a report's newest figures are.
#
# NOT UTC, ON PURPOSE. The UTC date rolls over at 8pm US Eastern in summer. A
# follow-up due tomorrow would read as overdue that same evening, and a report
# covering the day the manager is standing in would be announced as a day
# behind, every evening, for four or five hours.
#
# NOT THE HOST'S ZONE either: the host is a container in whatever region the
# platform put it in and has nothing to do with where the salons are.
#
# NEXT_PUBLIC_ so the server and the browser can never disagree about what day
# it is. It is not a secret — it is a timezone name.
#
# Unset is fine and is the normal case: the default is America/New_York. Set it
# only for a deployment that operates in a different zone, and set it to an
# IANA name (America/Chicago, America/Denver, America/Los_Angeles) rather than
# an offset, so daylight saving is handled by the platform rather than by us.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_BUSINESS_TIMEZONE=America/New_York

# ------------------------------------------ Report ingestion (machine auth) -
# SERVER ONLY, and deliberately not NEXT_PUBLIC_. Required on every call to
# POST /api/admin/reporting/ingest, in every environment. No credential means
# no ingestion — the route refuses everybody rather than falling open.
#
# A MACHINE credential, not a person's: it identifies a delivery pipeline,
# carries no profile or role, and must never be reused as employee login. It is
# production-capable on its own and depends on no external identity provider.
#
# FORMAT — one or more entries, comma or whitespace separated:
#     <secret>                       one credential, id defaults to `default`
#     <id>:<secret>                  one labelled credential
#     <id>:<secret>,<id>:<secret>    several, which is what rotation needs
#
# The id is an operator label (`power-automate`, `monthly-job`) and is not a
# secret: it is returned on a successful ingestion so an audit line can say
# which pipeline filed a report and which credential to revoke.
#
# ROTATE by adding the new entry, moving the caller over, then removing the old
# one — three deploys, no window where ingestion is broken. REVOKE by removing
# an entry and redeploying.
#
# Each secret must be at least 24 characters; a shorter entry is dropped and
# the readiness endpoint says so. Generate one, do not invent one:
#     openssl rand -base64 32
#
# Present it as `Authorization: Bearer <secret>` or in the
# `X-Reporting-Ingest-Secret` header. NEVER in a query string — a secret in a
# URL is written to every log between the caller and the route.
REPORTING_INGEST_SECRET=
# A SECOND ingestion credential, for a workbook a person files by hand.
# Accepted wherever REPORTING_INGEST_SECRET is, and neither is required — so a
# one-off manual upload never means touching the value the automation
# authenticates with. Same rules: at least 24 characters, or `id:secret`
# entries comma separated to hold several. Give it a value of its own; copying
# the automation's defeats the point, and the readiness endpoint says so.
REPORTING_MANUAL_INGEST_SECRET=

# ------------------------------ Google review sync (Brave extension auth) ---
# SERVER ONLY, and deliberately not NEXT_PUBLIC_. Required on every call to
# POST /api/reviews/ingest. No credential means no sync — the route refuses
# everybody rather than falling open.
#
# A MACHINE credential, exactly like REPORTING_INGEST_SECRET above, and held by
# the ASK Sunny Review Sync extension in an authorized person's Brave. It grants
# exactly one capability: to file Google reviews for the fifteen allowlisted
# Sun Tan City store codes. It carries no profile, no role and no scope, and it
# must never be reused as employee login.
#
# IT IS A SEPARATE VARIABLE FROM THE REPORTING ONE ON PURPOSE. A token typed
# into an Options page on somebody's laptop is a different exposure from a
# scheduled pipeline's, and the two must be revocable independently — sharing
# one value would mean pulling the extension's token takes report ingestion
# down with it.
#
# FORMAT — identical to REPORTING_INGEST_SECRET:
#     <secret>                       one credential, id defaults to
#                                    `brave-extension`
#     <id>:<secret>                  one labelled credential
#     <id>:<secret>,<id>:<secret>    several, which is what rotation needs
#
# Give each laptop its own labelled entry (`paulyne-laptop:...`) so one can be
# revoked without disturbing the others. The id is returned on a successful
# sync and shown in the extension, so an operator can confirm which credential
# a machine is using before revoking another.
#
# Each secret must be at least 24 characters. Generate one, do not invent one:
#     openssl rand -base64 32
#
# Presented as `Authorization: Bearer <token>` and never in a query string — a
# secret in a URL is written to every log between the caller and the route.
#
# PHASE 1: set this on the PREVIEW environment only. Production has no Google
# review sync until the preview QA is signed off.
GOOGLE_REVIEW_SYNC_SECRET=

# ------------------------- Google review sync (Apify, server-side source) ---
# THE PHASE 2 TRANSPORT. Apify runs a Google Maps reviews Actor on a schedule,
# on Apify's compute, and calls ASK Sunny back when it finishes — so reviews
# keep arriving with no laptop on, no Brave open, no Google tab and no Google
# login session. The Brave extension stays available as a fallback and as the
# way to check Apify's results by hand; nothing below turns it off.
#
# EVERY VARIABLE HERE IS SERVER ONLY. `APIFY_TOKEN` can start Actor runs and
# read every dataset on the account, so it is never NEXT_PUBLIC_, never given
# to the browser extension, never put in a frontend bundle, and never written
# into a URL. `src/lib/reviews/apify/config.ts` imports `server-only`, which
# makes a client component reaching it a build failure rather than a review
# comment.

# WHERE APIFY SHOULD CALL BACK. Deployment configuration, never a request's
# Host header — building a callback URL out of a header would let somebody who
# can reach the trigger point Apify's callback, and the secret attached to it,
# at a host of their choosing. Vercel's own VERCEL_URL is the fallback, which is
# what makes this work on a Preview with no stable site URL. It is already used
# by the password-recovery links, so a deployment that sends those has it set.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=

# The Apify API token, from Apify Console -> Settings -> API & Integrations.
# Required before any run can start.
APIFY_TOKEN=

# The Actor to run. Defaults to compass~google-maps-reviews-scraper, which was
# chosen for stable Google review ids, a real publication timestamp, many
# places per run, newest-first sorting and a date cutoff. `owner/name` is
# accepted and converted to Apify's `owner~name` form.
APIFY_ACTOR_ID=compass~google-maps-reviews-scraper

# The Actor that FINDS a listing, which is a different job and a different
# price. The reviews Actor above addresses a place it is given; it does not
# search. Discovery searches Google Maps once per salon from ASK Sunny's own
# roster, and places Actors are charged per PLACE returned rather than per
# review — so pointing one variable at both would silently change what a run
# costs.
#
# Discovery is a SETUP action, not a recurring one: once a listing is verified
# its Place ID is persisted and reused, and no scheduled path calls this Actor.
APIFY_PLACES_ACTOR_ID=compass~google-maps-extractor

# How many Google results to consider per salon during discovery. Default 5,
# maximum 20. It is the cost knob AND a safety feature: returning only the top
# hit would hide the second Sun Tan City in the same city and turn a genuine
# ambiguity into a confident wrong answer.
APIFY_DISCOVERY_CANDIDATES_PER_LOCATION=5

# THE MASTER SWITCH, AND IT IS OFF UNTIL SET. Neither the schedule nor the
# manual buttons will start a run while this is anything but true, so deploying
# this branch spends nothing.
APIFY_SYNC_ENABLED=false

# THE SCHEDULE'S OWN SWITCH, and it is off unless set.
#
# APIFY_SYNC_ENABLED above is the master switch: with it off, nothing anywhere
# reaches Apify. This one governs only the Vercel Cron tick, because QA needs a
# state one switch cannot express — manual Discovery and manual Sync working
# from the admin screen, while the schedule starts nothing.
#
# Without it, turning the integration on to test it for an afternoon also arms
# an unattended run at 6:00 the next morning, and the first anybody knows of it
# is the usage figure.
#
# WHAT IT ARMS: one run a day, at 6:00 AM US Central. `vercel.json` fires the
# route at 11:00 AND 12:00 UTC, which is one schedule written twice rather than
# two schedules — Vercel Cron is UTC-only, so 06:00 America/Chicago is 11:00 UTC
# under CDT and 12:00 UTC under CST. The route checks which of the two it is and
# the off-hour one starts nothing. See src/lib/reviews/apify/schedule.ts.
#
# Turn this on LAST, after watching a manual run do the right thing. The cron
# route still reconciles a run whose completion webhook was lost while this is
# off — that is a read of Apify's API, it starts nothing and costs nothing.
APIFY_SCHEDULE_ENABLED=false

# The credential Apify presents on the completion webhook. Same format and same
# 24-character minimum as GOOGLE_REVIEW_SYNC_SECRET above:
#     <secret> | <id>:<secret> | <id>:<secret>,<id>:<secret>
#     openssl rand -base64 32
#
# It is ONE OF THREE CHECKS, not the security model: the webhook must also name
# a run this system started and is still waiting on, and the results are then
# read from Apify's own API with APIFY_TOKEN rather than from the request. A
# forged webhook's best outcome is making ASK Sunny re-read a dataset it owns.
APIFY_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# The scheduled trigger's credential. Vercel sends it as `Authorization:
# Bearer <value>` on cron invocations. NOT OPTIONAL — without it the cron route
# refuses every call, including Vercel's, rather than leaving a public button
# that spends money.
CRON_SECRET=

# ---- The cost guardrails. All three are bounded in code and again in the DB.
#
# Reviews per location on the one-off historical import. Default 100, maximum
# 500 — which is MAX_REVIEWS_PER_SYNC, because a listing is handed to the
# ingestion in one batch so its feed positions stay comparable.
APIFY_REVIEW_BACKFILL_LIMIT_PER_LOCATION=100

# Reviews per location on each recurring run. Default 15: comfortably more than
# any of the fifteen listings receives between runs, so the reporting anchor
# stays inside the window. Deduplication is on Google's review id, so an
# overlapping window costs a few fetched records and creates nothing.
APIFY_REVIEW_INCREMENTAL_LIMIT_PER_LOCATION=15

# THE GUARDRAIL THAT MATTERS. A wrong per-location limit costs a multiple; a
# loop that starts runs costs without bound. Checked inside the same
# transaction that takes the single-run lock, so no route can skip it.
# Default 8, rolling 24 hours.
APIFY_MAX_RUNS_PER_DAY=8

# How far back a recurring run's date cutoff reaches before the oldest listing's
# newest review. Closes the gap around a run boundary at the cost of a handful
# of already-known records. Default 6 hours.
APIFY_INCREMENTAL_OVERLAP_HOURS=6

# Bounds on the Actor run itself, sent to Apify so a hung run stops costing
# money at the timeout rather than at the plan's limit.
APIFY_RUN_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=900
APIFY_RUN_MEMORY_MBYTES=2048

# Operator-written, shown as "Scheduled sync" on the source screen. Never
# parsed — it describes the cron entry in vercel.json, it does not set it.
#
# LEAVE IT UNSET. The panel then reads the description straight out of
# `schedule.ts`, which is the same module the cron route decides with, so it
# cannot go stale. Setting it overrides that with free text, and free text
# describing a schedule it does not control is how a panel ends up advertising
# last quarter's timings. A deployment that already has the old value should
# CLEAR this variable rather than retype it.
APIFY_SYNC_SCHEDULE=

# PHASE 2: set all of these on the PREVIEW environment only. Production has no
# Apify sync until the preview QA and the Brave-versus-Apify reconciliation are
# signed off.

# ------------------------------------- Google review weekly goal ------------
# THE ONE NUMBER BEHIND THE OVERVIEW'S REVIEW METER, and the only place a
# review target exists anywhere in this app.
#
# The weekly target for ONE SALON. The Overview's "This week" block multiplies
# it by the salons the Google review system holds, so the estate's goal moves
# with the estate instead of being frozen at whatever the roster was the day
# somebody typed a total. Fifteen salons at 15 each is a goal of 225.
#
# UNSET IS THE DEFAULT AND IS A REAL STATE. With no value the block draws NO
# meter and NO percentage, and prints an em dash under "Weekly goal" — the
# count, the average rating and the week-over-week change are all still real.
# That is deliberate: nothing in Supabase holds a review goal, and the 230 this
# replaced was the sum of invented per-salon goals in the seeded demo module.
# A meter against a number nobody agreed to is a figure a manager quotes in a
# meeting. See docs/google-reviews-phase-1.md §6.
#
# A whole number of reviews. 0 means "no target this week" and draws no meter,
# exactly like unset. Anything that is not a whole number — a decimal, a word,
# a negative — is treated as UNSET rather than as a default, because a target
# the deployment did not choose is the failure this variable exists to avoid.
#
# Server-only on purpose: it is read where the figures are read, so it can be
# changed on the deployment without a rebuild.
GOOGLE_REVIEWS_WEEKLY_GOAL_PER_SALON=

# ------------------------------------- Inbound report email (Resend) --------
# The report arrives as a forwarded email rather than an HTTP call:
#
#   Samuel emails Curt the Comp Report
#     -> an Outlook rule forwards a copy to the Resend inbound address
#     -> Resend emits `email.received`
#     -> POST /api/reporting/inbound-email verifies, gates, downloads the .xlsx
#     -> the same intake orchestration runs every applicable parser
#
# All three are SERVER ONLY and deliberately not NEXT_PUBLIC_.

# The webhook signing secret Resend shows ONCE when the webhook is created.
# Starts `whsec_`. Every delivery is verified against it over the RAW request
# body before anything is parsed, downloaded or written; a runtime without it
# refuses every delivery rather than trusting one.
RESEND_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# A Resend API key with permission to read received email. The webhook carries
# attachment METADATA only, so the bytes are fetched from the receiving API.
# Sent to api.resend.com and nowhere else — never to an attachment's signed
# download URL, which points at a different host.
RESEND_API_KEY=

# Comma-separated list of addresses whose mail may become reporting data.
# EXACT addresses, case and whitespace normalized. No domain wildcards: a rule
# like "anything at the company" would let any colleague file figures by
# replying to the thread.
#
#   REPORTING_APPROVED_SENDERS=you@yourcompany.com,Samuel.Brockie@glowbrands.com
#
# Not authentication — a From address is trivially forged, and the webhook
# SIGNATURE is what proves a delivery is genuine. This is the narrower question
# of whose report we agreed to ingest. Unset means nobody is admitted.
# Removing the test address later is an edit here, not a code change.
REPORTING_APPROVED_SENDERS=

# ------------------------------------------- Sales Totals email ingestion ---
# The SAME inbound mailbox and the SAME webhook as the Comp Report. One
# endpoint serves both; they are told apart by sender and subject.
#
#   the reporting system emails the Sales Totals report
#     -> an Outlook rule forwards a copy to the same Resend inbound address
#     -> POST /api/reporting/inbound-email verifies, routes, downloads
#     -> the Sales Totals parser reads it and one transaction writes the day
#
# Comma-separated EXACT addresses, as above. No domain wildcards. This is the
# ONLY thing standing between the report and ingestion, so unset admits nobody
# — which is why it is configuration rather than a default in the code: a test
# address has to be removable without a release.
#
#   SALES_TOTALS_APPROVED_SENDERS=reports@suntancity.com
#
SALES_TOTALS_APPROVED_SENDERS=

# OPTIONAL. The subject fragment a Sales Totals delivery must carry, matched
# case-insensitively with whitespace collapsed. Defaults to "sales totals",
# which matches the observed subject ("Sales Totals for Bowen"); set it only to
# narrow further, e.g. to one recipient's copy.
SALES_TOTALS_SUBJECT_FRAGMENT=

# ---------------------------------------- Reporting access: NONE -----------
# THERE IS NO VARIABLE HERE, AND THAT IS THE POINT.
#
# /reports/salon-performance renders real salon-level financials and NOTHING
# GUARDS IT. The temporary stakeholder-review password gate that used to stand
# in front of it was removed at the owner's explicit instruction, and the app's
# own sign-in is demo mode — anybody can click "Preview demo" and is inside.
#
# So the reporting dashboard is readable by anyone who has the URL, on every
# environment this is deployed to. Treat the deployment hostname as the only
# thing standing between a competitor and comparable-store revenue by location.
#
# THE FIX IS EMPLOYEE LOGIN, not another shared password. Supabase Auth is the
# default choice — see docs/architecture-constraints.md §3. Until it ships,
# limit who has the URL.

# ------------------------------------------------------------ Supabase -----
# Project URL. Public by design.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=

# Publishable key (`sb_publishable_...`) — browser-safe, protected by row level
# security. Not read by any code path yet; it becomes required when
# authentication ships and the browser starts talking to Supabase directly.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=

# SERVER ONLY. Secret key (`sb_secret_...`) — bypasses row level security
# entirely. Used exclusively inside route handlers for ingestion, signed
# download URLs, and calling the `embed` Edge Function.
# Never expose this to the browser. Never prefix it with NEXT_PUBLIC_.
SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY=

# Legacy alternative, still accepted if SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY is unset. Supabase
# is retiring the anon/service_role JWT keys in favour of the publishable/secret
# pair above; a newly created project is issued both. Prefer SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY.
# SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=

# ------------------------------------------------------------- Claude ------
# SERVER ONLY. Read in src/lib/ai/anthropic.ts, which is marked `server-only`.
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

# Optional overrides. The defaults live in src/lib/config/models.ts.
# ANTHROPIC_MODEL=
# ANTHROPIC_EFFORT=

# --------------------------------------------------- Authentication --------
# SERVER ONLY, and normally unset.
#
# Live mode refuses protected functionality until a real identity provider is
# configured. Setting this to exactly "true" bypasses that refusal, for the one
# pre-authentication acceptance test that proves upload -> retrieval -> answer
# works against real credentials.
#
# IT CANNOT OPERATE IN PRODUCTION. The bypass is gated on NODE_ENV, so in a
# production build (`next build` / `next start`, and therefore every real
# deployment) it is inert whatever it is set to. Setting it there changes
# nothing except a SECURITY line in the log telling you it was ignored.
#
# Usable only under `next dev` or the test runner.
# ALLOW_UNAUTHENTICATED_LIVE_ACCESS=

# --------------------------------------------------------- Embeddings ------
# NO VARIABLE REQUIRED.
#
# Documents and questions are embedded by the `gte-small` model running inside
# the project's own Supabase Edge Function (supabase/functions/embed). There is
# no embedding vendor and no embedding API key: the Supabase URL and secret key
# above are the whole credential set.
#
# The model and its output dimension are set in src/lib/config/models.ts and
# must stay in step with the pgvector column width in supabase/migrations.

# ------------------------------------------------- Training destinations ---
# PUBLIC, and both OPTIONAL. Added by the 14 September stakeholder review:
#
#   "Recommended Training is currently an empty section. Since our training
#    videos live in Teams and Woven, I'd rather this be a link directing users
#    to those resources than an empty section."
#
# NO URL IS INVENTED IN CODE. A Teams channel link and a Woven workspace link
# are facts about the customer's tenancy, and a plausible guess would produce a
# link that looks right, goes nowhere, and is found by a Salon Director rather
# than by a developer.
#
# UNSET IS A SUPPORTED STATE. The Overview then says training lives in Teams and
# Woven and that this deployment has not been given their addresses — an honest
# empty state, not a dead link and not a blank panel.
#
# Must be ABSOLUTE http(s) URLs. A relative value is refused, because it would
# resolve against Ask Sunny's own origin and link to a page that is not here.
# See src/lib/config/training-links.ts.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_TEAMS_TRAINING_URL=
# NEXT_PUBLIC_WOVEN_TRAINING_URL=

# --------------------------------------------- Non-production form records --
# SERVER ONLY, OPTIONAL, and EMPTY BY DEFAULT — it excludes nothing until set.
#
# The review found test records in the Overview's follow-up queue. Records filed
# against a salon that is not on the roster are already excluded structurally,
# by name and by salon id; this covers what that cannot reach — a test employee
# filed against a real salon, or against no salon at all.
#
# Comma-separated employee names, compared case- and whitespace-insensitively.
# NO NAME IS COMPILED INTO THE PRODUCT: a name in a source file is a guess that
# ages badly and cannot be changed without a deploy, while a name here is a
# decision an administrator owns and can reverse.
#
# NOTHING IS DELETED OR HIDDEN FROM ITS OWN SCREEN. Form Monitoring still shows
# every record; only the Overview's summary queue is filtered.
#
# The records this deployment currently needs are listed in
# docs/test-data-cleanup.md, with archiving offered as the reversible
# alternative.
# ASK_SUNNY_EXCLUDED_EMPLOYEE_NAMES=
